egime was a  limited space of  freedoms that he gave so the concern is  from the security agency's point of view  right now is that any criticism can very  easily snowball into major street  protests that in turn would endanger  stability  of  regime or government depending on who  you ask of course  but this essentially is the issue is  that in order to  prevent any sort of  effective opposition security forces  have been silencing all  the critical voices and you see that for  example in targeting not just um you  know not just major uh political  opponents but really going after  individuals over facebook  posts going after um lawyers for example  which is also assigned as the  authorities want to further prevent  lawyers and prevent  others from reporting violations that  are happening  in the country so most recently we saw  the arrest of yusuf monsoor uh 20 uh  lawyer in his twenties simply because he  posted uh on his social media page about  um  and now he's detained pending  investigation over terrorism and charges  so in short the government really  doesn't prosecute isis in particular see  any sort of criticism as a big or as a  first step towards anti-government  mobilization  muhammad i want to talk more  specifically for a moment about the  sentence that was given to ahmad taha  this is a 15-year jail sentence that was  given in absentia for spreading false  news over a 2018 interview with abdomen  he's a leading opposition figure and  former presidential candidate in egypt  himself was sentenced to 15 years in  jail this week over accusations of  plotting against the state  even in a country where  the jailing and sentencing of of  journalists and dissidents has become  quite common  does this action this sentencing of  ahmed taha to 15 years  in jail for this interview does that not  seem excessive  well i mean what doesn't uh  seem excessive you know uh  cc came to power  in 2013 on the back of a military coup  uh the violence that was carried out at  the time was was excessive there were  five large-scale massacres of civilians  carried out in the summer of 2013.  the regime went on to eliminate all  forms of political opposition they  arrested tens of thousands of people  including political leaders they banned  all of the key opposition parties they  shut down all of the key opposition news  outlets so you know what in that was it  wasn't excessive so you know um this is  this is an extreme authoritarian regime  so while it's shocking to us that that a  journalist could be uh you know uh  sentenced to 15 years in jail for  quote-unquote false news spreading false  news just for asking some questions in  an interview really it's entirely uh  consistent with with uh with the the  sisi regime's uh modis uh apparanda and  i just wanted to piggyback on uh what mr  bayumi was saying on the previous  question  you know in terms of you know why the  regime acts out the way that it does  this is a very scared very paranoid  regime in addition to the things that i  just mentioned about eliminationism  you know we have to remember that assisi  is deeply insecure  a few people tried to  you know put their names in the hat  and run against him  in the presidential election a few years  ago and he arrested them uh or otherwise  eliminated them so that just underscored  the extent to which uh he's he's  insecure and that's one of the reasons  why  egypt does not tolerate  any sign of dissent whatsoever i thought  philip luther  amnesty's middle eastern north africa  director said it best a couple of years  ago when he said the egyptian  authorities have made it very clear that  anyone who challenges the official  narrative will be severely uh punished  and if there's an opportunity i think we  should talk at some point today about um  nabila makram uh who  is the immigration minister who  threatened egyptians abroad  with death  a few years back in in a speech in in  canada  saying that any egyptian who speaks  against the state who criticizes the  government uh could be could be killed  could be sliced up uh in her in her  words  shadi  what legal justification is there for  egyptian courts to dole out these kinds  of verdicts and sentences what is the  rationale that they are going by and  also from your point of view is there  any space that currently exists in egypt  for for opposition or for dissent or for  critical voices  well not at all i think what what we saw  is the same promises that lccc have  given over and over and never delivered  on  we might see  some gestures just to  allow  some of those  things to happen  this year including while they host the  international environment  conference in uh sharmishir in september  of course it's an opportunity for the  government to try and get gain some  support as it tackles its economic uh  challenges and immediate threats  so of course there is an opening an  opportunity that can materialize but  those are legal battles that  no one has control of because they are  happening in emergency  courts  those who have issued even death penalty  against some al jazeera journalists in  the past and some who continue to be  jailed today  in pretrial detention without three  course without evidence some for four  years or more so yes there isn't any  legal ability to find justice within  egypt's border and that's why many  people are demanding justin to happen uh  in a political price or even in u.s  courts or  european parliament courts not just in  congress and in other ways so yes we  want to see a lot more uh focus on the  right of dissidence approval but also  shed light on people who do not get even  the attention that al jazeera  journalists have there are four more  jesus journalists who are imprisoned in  egypt today  but there are so many who do not even  have  any affiliation with an outlet let alone  a regional or an international one  all right well we have run out of time  so we're going to have to leave the  conversation 